  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1016 AM MDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nWINDY AND MOSTLY DRY THROUGH FRIDAY: \n\nThe current synoptic level pattern is characterized by a trough \nmoving into the PacNW and a ridge beginning to develop over the \nSouthern Plains. As a result of this building trough, tightening \npressure gradient contours aloft will make for stronger winds aloft. \nThis, in addition to ample daytime heating today and Friday will \nmake for periods of those stronger winds mixing down to the surface. \nHence, afternoon gusts of 25-35 mph are expected for parts of the \narea, with the highest coverage of gusts being north of I-70. Dry \nair works its way into the area today, keeping precipitation chances \npretty low to finish off the workweek. However, some isolated \nafternoon convection should not be ruled out completely for the \nhigher terrain areas today and Friday. \n\nRETURN OF MOISTURE FOR THE HOLIDAY WEEKEND: \n\nAs we become sandwiched between the aformentioned troughing to our \nnorthwest and ridging to our southeast, flow aloft will be mostly \nsouthwesterly. This continues to be favorable flow for moisture \nadvection into our area. Each day, we should see an increase in \nshower and storm chances, with Sunday and Monday being the peak in \nprecipitation coverage. Models are also hinting at shortwaves \nrotating around the periphery of the PacNW low, which could lead to \nincreased forcing, further enhancing precipitation development. \nAdditionally, models are in agreement that PWATs upwards of 200% of \nnormal are expected for much of the area. Because of all this, the \npotential for heavy rainfall exists, therefore the potential for \nflash flooding and debris flows also exists. This threat is \nparticularly heightened where soils have reached peak saturation \nover the past few rounds of showers and storms. \n\nBeyond Monday, model outcomes begin to diverge from each other. The \nGFS appears to be heavy handed bringing in a second tropical system \nover the Baja Peninsula late next week, which most other models do \nnot have a strong signal on. So at this time, there is uncertainty \nwith how long we hold onto moisture chances next week and therefore \nforecast confidence remains low beyond Monday. As for temperatures, \ndaytime highs appear to hover around normal, if not below at times \nof cloud cover and precipitation.",
